
class Solution {
    public boolean isPalindrome(ListNode head) {
        
        // Edge case: empty list or single node is always palindrome
        if (head == null || head.next == null) return true;

        // Step 1: Find the middle of the linked list
        ListNode slow = head;   // moves 1 step
        ListNode fast = head;   // moves 2 steps
        
        // When fast reaches end, slow will be at middle
        while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {
            slow = slow.next;
            fast = fast.next.next;
        }

        // Step 2: Reverse the second half of the list starting from 'slow'
        ListNode secondHalf = reverse(slow);

        // Step 3: Compare first half and reversed second half
        ListNode firstHalf = head;
        ListNode temp = secondHalf;
        
        // Compare node values one by one
        while (temp != null) {
            if (firstHalf.val != temp.val) {
                return false; // mismatch → not palindrome
            }
            firstHalf = firstHalf.next;
            temp = temp.next;
        }

        // If all values matched
        return true;
    }

    // Helper function to reverse a linked list
    private ListNode reverse(ListNode head) {
        ListNode prev = null;
        
        while (head != null) {
            ListNode next = head.next; // store next node
            head.next = prev;          // reverse link
            prev = head;               // move prev forward
            head = next;               // move head forward
        }
        
        return prev; // new head of reversed list
    }
}
🔍 Dry Run (Example)
Input:
1 → 2 → 2 → 1
🟡 Step 1: Find Middle
Iteration	slow	fast
start	1	1
1	2	2
2	2	null

👉 slow is now at middle (3rd node)

🟡 Step 2: Reverse Second Half

Starting from slow = 2 → 1

After reversing:

1 → 2
🟡 Step 3: Compare Halves
First Half	Second Half
1	1 ✅
2	2 ✅

👉 All matched → Palindrome

⚠️ Important Note (Interview Insight)

For odd-length list like:

1 → 2 → 3 → 2 → 1
slow lands at 3
Reversing from 3 still works because middle element doesn’t affect palindrome
🔁 Optional Improvement (Restore List)

In interviews, sometimes they expect you to restore the list:

reverse(secondHalf);

(Not required for LeetCode, but good practice.)

🧠 Key Intuition
Use fast & slow pointers → find middle
Reverse only half list → save space
Compare → done
